  $2 = 1      row count
              the generic plan

  PostgreSQL would switch to the generic plan after five executions, whatever their values: it
  estimates the generic plan at 1,597, below the custom plan of every value tried (from 1,602 to
  5,100 with planning).
  → Have PostgreSQL plan each execution for its values: set plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan for
  the application's connections (in a JDBC URL: options=-c%20plan_cache_mode=force_custom_plan) or
  its role (ALTER ROLE … SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan). Each execution is then planned
  again.
  → Or keep the driver from preparing the statement on the server: prepareThreshold=0 with pgJDBC,
  for the connection or for this statement (PGStatement.setPrepareThreshold(0)); prepare_threshold =
  None with psycopg 3.
  Values come from the column statistics, or are common row counts for a LIMIT or an OFFSET, and are
  tried one parameter at a time, the others held at a typical value; how columns depend on each
  other is not taken into account.
  The statement's JDBC placeholders (?) were read as $1, $2, …

The plan shown is the generic plan, measured with $1 = 10001, $2 = 10, the values it does worst
with.

67.1 ms. 100% of it in Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on orders, which finds 164,414 rows
through orders_created_at_idx and its filter throws away 164,404.

Planning 0.200 ms · Execution 67.1 ms · Buffers 176,778 pages, 100% from cache

 Share      Time              Node                                                 Rows  Estimate  Buffers
0.018%  0.012 ms              Limit                                                  10    1 ▲10×        0
  100%   67.1 ms  ██████████  └─ Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on orders    10        10  176,778

Findings (1)

  HIGH    ES006 Index scan that filters most rows
          Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on orders finds 164,414 rows through
          orders_created_at_idx and its filter throws away 164,404.
          Index: orders_created_at_idx
          Filter: (customer_id = $1)
          Rows removed by the filter: 164,404 of 164,414 found
          → An index on orders that starts with customer_id and continues with the columns of
          orders_created_at_idx would let the index do the filtering.

Advice (1)

  LIKELY  Index orders (customer_id)
          CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON orders (customer_id);
          An index on orders (customer_id) would let the index do the filtering that Index Scan
          using orders_created_at_idx on orders now does on every row it finds.
          ! Not connected to the database: existing indexes, the table's write load and the column
          statistics were not checked.
          ! The condition compares with a value known only at run time, so whether the planner uses
          the index depends on that value.

Share and Time: time spent in each node itself, wall-clock. Rows: actual rows per loop, ×loops when
it ran more than once. ▲/▼: actual rows above or below the estimate by 10× or more. Buffers: pages
each node read itself.
